@Article(time: 5) {
  @Intro(title: "Deployment") {
    One static binary and one SQLite file. The artifact is the static musl build, which is the answer
    that the whole dependency stack links statically, so the machine that runs it needs no toolchain,
    no interpreter and no service beyond what this page names.

    The data is the file `DATABASE_URL` names, in the directory the mount and the state directory
    name as the same place, and the port the run answers on is the port the address value names.
    The policy the two deployment files name is read as one value, and the signal the service file
    names is a signal the process handler has a case for, which is the clean stop path answering 0.

    ```sh
    rivulet migrate
    rivulet backup /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet backup --verify /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet backup --force /var/backups/rivulet.db
    rivulet vacuum
    rivulet serve
    ```

    The copy is `VACUUM INTO`: the snapshot at the moment of the run, written as a file of its own
    that does not change with the source afterwards. Before the write the run reads the room at the
    directory the destination is in, and asks for the bytes the source holds as the filesystem reads
    them, plus the bytes the WAL holds, times the factor it uses (2). A destination the run did not
    create is reported, not built; a destination that exists is reported rather than overwritten
    unless `--force` names it, and that flag does not skip the room read. `--verify` opens the copy
    the run wrote through its own connection and reports the problems it finds, leaving the copy as
    written. `vacuum` rebuilds the file it already holds, so it reads no room.

    There is no restore command: the recipe is the file swap, which is why the copy is a plain file
    rather than a database the run can open. Stop the run that keeps running, copy the copy back to
    the path `DATABASE_URL` names, run `migrate`, and read the counts back with `status --json`.

    The storage wrapper sets synchronous to `NORMAL`, so committed writes are in the operating
    system's cache rather than forced to the device per row: the recovery point is one round, not one
    entry, and the round is the batch the tick takes. The checkpoint that empties the WAL runs
    best-effort at the end of a run, so the bytes a run leaves in the WAL are the interrupted round's
    when a stop lands mid-round.

    The schedule is read as whole Unix seconds and the cadence as minutes, so a clock that moves by
    more than the interval moves the schedule for every row. Adjust the time incrementally and read
    the clock after the first round rather than before it.

    An upgrade replaces the binary and runs `migrate`. Until the first release the schema is edited in
    place, so an upgrade that changes the schema is not something the run can apply to an existing
    database. Streaming the file to another device is a tool outside this port: no command of the port
    runs it, and nothing here reads what a stream of it would hold.
  }
}
